Let teardown wait for control senders. Java threads send controller audio, display and
reconfigure messages without being joined; once teardown closes sending no new send starts,
and the peer and host are destroyed only after every sender in flight has left.
Sending reopens only at the end of initializeControlStream(), once the mutex, crypto contexts
and sequence number belong to the new stream, and destroyControlStream() closes it as well,
since a stream whose start failed before it had a peer is never stopped.
LiGetEstimatedRttInfo() is also called from Java threads the stream never joins (the stats
overlay, and Game's 1 s bitrate, quality and health timers on the main thread), and it read
the peer with no lock while teardown could destroy the host that holds it. It now counts as a sender, returns false once
sending is closed, and reads the peer under enetMutex. That lock is normally held briefly,
but the control receive thread holds it for up to about a second while it waits for a
held-back host disconnect, so an RTT read in that window can block its caller that long.
Applies after control-payload.patch.

